# Kyverno - Default Resource Policy

## 1. Install Kyverno

```bash
helm repo add kyverno https://kyverno.github.io/kyverno

helm repo update

helm install kyverno kyverno/kyverno \
  --namespace kyverno \
  --create-namespace \
  --set replicaCount=1
````

Check Kyverno:

```bash
kubectl get pods -n kyverno --watch
```

---

## 2. Test Pod Before Policy

Create Pod:

```bash
kubectl run before --image=nginx
```

Check Pod:

```bash
kubectl get pods
```

Describe Pod:

```bash
kubectl describe pod before
```

### Result

Since no CPU or memory requests are defined:

```text
QoS Class: BestEffort
```

---

## 3. Create Kyverno Policy

Create file:

```bash
vi policy.yml
```

### policy.yml

```yaml
apiVersion: kyverno.io/v1
kind: ClusterPolicy

metadata:
  name: add-default-resources

  annotations:
    policies.kyverno.io/title: Add Default Resources
    policies.kyverno.io/category: Other
    policies.kyverno.io/severity: medium
    kyverno.io/kyverno-version: 1.10.0-alpha.2
    policies.kyverno.io/minversion: 1.7.0
    policies.kyverno.io/kubernetes-version: "1.26"
    policies.kyverno.io/subject: Pod
    policies.kyverno.io/description: >-
      Pods which don't specify at least resource requests are assigned a QoS class
      of BestEffort which can hog resources for other Pods on Nodes. At a minimum,
      all Pods should specify resource requests in order to be labeled as the QoS
      class Burstable. This sample mutates any container in a Pod which doesn't
      specify memory or cpu requests to apply some sane defaults.

spec:
  background: false

  rules:
    - name: add-default-requests

      match:
        any:
          - resources:
              kinds:
                - Pod

      preconditions:
        any:
          - key: "{{request.operation || 'BACKGROUND'}}"
            operator: AnyIn
            value:
              - CREATE
              - UPDATE

      mutate:
        foreach:
          - list: "request.object.spec.containers[]"

            patchStrategicMerge:
              spec:
                containers:
                  - (name): "{{element.name}}"

                    resources:
                      requests:
                        +(memory): "100Mi"
                        +(cpu): "100m"
```

---

## 4. Apply Policy

```bash
kubectl apply -f policy.yml
```

Check policy:

```bash
kubectl get clusterpolicy
```

---

## 5. Test Pod After Policy

Create another Pod:

```bash
kubectl run after --image=nginx
```

Check Pods:

```bash
kubectl get pods
```

Describe the Pod:

```bash
kubectl describe pod after
```

---

## 6. Verify the Result

The `after` Pod should now have:

```text
Requests:
  cpu:     100m
  memory:  100Mi
```

And:

```text
QoS Class: Burstable
```

You can also verify using:

```bash
kubectl get pod after -o yaml
```

---

## 7. Before vs After

| Pod      | CPU Request | Memory Request | QoS          |
| -------- | ----------- | -------------- | ------------ |
| `before` | Not defined | Not defined    | `BestEffort` |
| `after`  | `100m`      | `100Mi`        | `Burstable`  |

---

## 8. How It Works

```text
Pod Created
    |
    v
Kyverno checks Pod
    |
    v
Checks each container
    |
    v
CPU request missing?
    └── Yes → Add 100m

Memory request missing?
    └── Yes → Add 100Mi

    |
    v
Pod gets resource requests
    |
    v
QoS = Burstable
```

---

## 9. Important Part

```yaml
resources:
  requests:
    +(memory): "100Mi"
    +(cpu): "100m"
```

`+` means **add the value only if it does not already exist**.

So an existing request will not be overwritten.

---

## 10. Cleanup

```bash
kubectl delete pod before after

kubectl delete clusterpolicy add-default-resources

helm uninstall kyverno -n kyverno

kubectl delete namespace kyverno
```

